    "summary": "runTeam in src/cli/command.ts now handles `team claim|release <epicHash> [storyId [taskId]]`: parseClaimUnit accepts a 16-hex epic hash, then an optional story id (s<digits>) and optional task id (t<digits>, only with a story), lowercases them, and returns an epic/story/task unit; anything else (missing, malformed, extra args, task without story) returns the usage line, which now lists claim|release and still begins 'usage: team init'. The unit goes through the services layer: new svc.team.teamClaim / teamRelease in src/cli/services/team.ts (wired in services/index.ts) call the gate's claim / release for the selected repo and print '✓ claimed|released <unit> <hash>/<story>/<task>' or '✗ not claimed|released — <describeOutcome>' (so a held stage prints 'held by bob', a non-holder release prints the only-the-holder reason, a repo without claims says so). Help text has the new line; the test fakes in command.test.ts and tui.test.ts gained the two methods. The new test team-claim-release.test.ts drives the real services with injected gate dependencies and shows: epic, story and task units reach the gate resolved correctly; claim adds the actor through addAssignee only; a bob-held claim prints 'held by bob' and adds nothing; holder release clears, stranger release is refused with no clear, administrator release clears; a no-policy repo says it does not enforce claims; seven bad-argument forms print the usage line and ask the gate nothing. All 98 cli tests pass.",
